  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Maxim Shubarev is a Russian property developer and the founder and chairman of Setl Group, a major housebuilder based in St Petersburg. Trained at the Leningrad Polytechnic Institute, he began working as a radio-equipment assembler and served in the Soviet Navy before building his fortune through residential and commercial development.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 95,
      "band": "very_high",
      "reason": "Trade association,industry biography and dedicated wealth profile corroborate durable background."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S3",
      "S6"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Maxim Shubarev is a Russian property developer and the founder and chairman of Setl Group, a major housebuilder based in St Petersburg. Trained at the Leningrad Polytechnic Institute, he began working as a radio-equipment assembler and served in the Soviet Navy before building his fortune through residential and commercial development.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Maxim Shubarev entered property development by helping finish housing begun for employees of a former state enterprise. Following privatisation, the project needed money to complete construction. Private purchasers supplied additional funds, allowing both the original applicants and the new buyers to receive apartments. Shubarev and his partner Yan Izak then established Petersburg Real Estate.\n\nThe business that became Setl Group also worked on buildings with very different purposes. Its reconstruction projects included the Konstantinovsky Palace, Mikhailovsky Castle and the General Staff arch in St Petersburg. It contributed to the university management-school campus at Mikhailovskaya Dacha, combining commercial construction with work on the city’s architectural inheritance.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 91,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Full historical business entry and primary industry association distinguish project financing from subsequent company reconstruction work."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S4",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 105,
    "html": "<p>Maxim Shubarev entered property development by helping finish housing begun for employees of a former state enterprise. Following privatisation, the project needed money to complete construction. Private purchasers supplied additional funds, allowing both the original applicants and the new buyers to receive apartments. Shubarev and his partner Yan Izak then established Petersburg Real Estate.</p>\r\n<p>The business that became Setl Group also worked on buildings with very different purposes. Its reconstruction projects included the Konstantinovsky Palace, Mikhailovsky Castle and the General Staff arch in St Petersburg. It contributed to the university management-school campus at Mikhailovskaya Dacha, combining commercial construction with work on the city’s architectural inheritance.</p>\r\n"
  },
